        "heading_igXbXd": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Number Behind Every Fish He Chooses",
            "heading_size": "title-lg tracking-heading",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_TyNqBx": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Most adults test in the \"danger zone,\" a 4 to 5 percent Omega-3 Index against a healthy range of 8 to 12 percent, according to OmegaQuant's population data. Nick has spent years pushing his own index well past that range (currently sitting at 15.68%), and the way he gets there comes down to one principle:<strong> bioaccumulation. </strong></p><p>Mercury enters the food chain at the bottom and concentrates as it moves up, so bigger, longer-lived fish carry more of it. He'll point out that a single serving of swordfish carries as much mercury as 77 servings of sardines, which is why species selection, not the label, decides whether a high-omega diet is actually safe.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
